  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe primary approach involves leveraging adaptive resolution optimization combined with a hybrid global-local search strategy. Unlike the previous SLSQP-based optimization, this method uses a coarse-to-fine resolution scheme that first identifies promising solution topologies at low resolutions and then refines them at higher resolutions. The key innovation is a multi-level neighborhood search that combines gradient information with stochastic exploration to escape local optima. This addresses the core bottleneck of premature convergence in high-dimensional optimization.\n\nThe method first searches a low-resolution representation (n=60) to identify promising topological features. Then, it projects these solutions to higher resolutions (n=120, then 240) while maintaining the core structure. The objective approximation uses a smoothed version of the original max-overlap problem with adaptive smoothing parameters. Randomized perturbations inject diversity, while gradient information ensures refinement stays close to good regions.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nThe problem is modeled as:\nMinimize C\u2085(N) = max_{k} \u222b_0^2 h(x)[1-h(x+k)] dx\nSubject to 0 \u2264 h \u2264 1 and \u222b_0^2 h dx = 1\n\nAt resolution N:\nVariables: h \u2208 \u211d^N, 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211 h[i] = N/2\nApproximated objective: \nO(h, \u03b1) = ln( \u2211_k exp(\u03b1 * ( -C\u2085\\_discrete(h, k) ) )\nWhere C\u2085\\_discrete(h, k) is the discretized overlap integral.\n\nConstraints are enforced via projection:\ndef project_to_sum(h, target_sum):\n    diff = target_sum - np.sum(h)\n    if |diff| < 1e-14: return h\n    else: \n        mask = (h > 1e-12) & (h < 1.0-1e-12)\n        if mask.any(): \n            h[...] += diff / np.sum(mask)\n            return np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n        else: \n            h += diff / len(h)\n            return np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n\nSmoothed objective gradient is computed via:\ndef smooth_max_and_grad(h, alpha):\n    Compute correlation array corr\n    Subtract max for illumination\n    Use exponential tilting for gradient\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\nPhase 0: Initialization\n- Set up resolution progression: [60, 120, 240]\n- Ensure h array is float32 to minimize memory\n- Track best solution across all resolutions\n\nPhase 1: Coarse resolution search (n=60)\n- Generate initial population: uniform sampling + sine harmonics\n- Use adaptive DE variant with fitness = O(h, \u03b1)\n- Store top 5 solutions from this phase\n\nPhase 2: Progressive resolution refinement\n- For each good solution from Phase 1:\n  - Scale to next resolution (N -> 2N) via spline interpolation\n  - Run gradient-based refinement at higher resolution\n  - Apply guided random mutation using gradient information\n\nPhase 3: Nelder-Mead polishing phase\n- Use fmin_cobyla for constraint-enforced optimization\n- Incorporate worst-case restarts (10% of budget) to prevent stalling\n\nPhase 4: Final random-scan validation\n- Run extensive objective evaluation across solution neighborhood\n- Verify no potential improvement remains within error tolerance\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n\nResolution progression: n_list = [240, 180, 120, 60, 30, 20]  # 240 is parent, 180 is fallback, finish with 120, then 60?\n\u03b1 schedule: [20, 50, 200] for different resolutions\nMutation rate: 0.7 at coarse, 0.3 at fine\nGradient step size: 0.01 * max(h) * (n_points**(-0.5))  # adaptive to resolution\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n\nTotal iterations bounded by:\n- For n=60: max 1200 evaluations\n- For n=120: max 800 evaluations\n- For n=240: max 600 evaluations\n\nDeadline catcher: monitor elapsed time at beginning of each call, return best solution when budget exhausted\n\nHandles overdetermined systems by using chebyshev projection:\nh_final = np.clip( solve(A*x=B), 0, 1 )\nWhere A is chosen to satisfy integral constraints\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n- Compute objective with numpy.correlate(mode='full') and dx=2/n_points\n- Return solution clipped to [0,1] (though project_to_sum should ensure validity)\n- Include sanity checks for gradient magnitude and objective smoothness\n- Use finite-differences on random samples to ensure gradients are not nan/inf\n\nThe construction satisfies constraints through:\n1. project_to_sum adaptive clipping\n2. Gradient-based iterations clipping\n3. Gradient initialization bounds enforcement",
